WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.463
(piano noises)

00:00:04.979 --> 00:00:06.170
- I've always been inspired

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.117
or fascinated by weather.

00:00:08.117 --> 00:00:10.608
What does a cloud sound like?

00:00:10.608 --> 00:00:12.207
You know, even as a child, I can remember

00:00:12.207 --> 00:00:13.837
sitting out on the grass and looking up

00:00:13.837 --> 00:00:16.646
at the clouds as they go past.

00:00:16.646 --> 00:00:19.197
What kind of sound would
those clouds output

00:00:19.197 --> 00:00:22.947
using a piano if they
rolled across the keys?

00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:28.218
My name is David Bowen.

00:00:28.218 --> 00:00:30.848
I'm a studio artist and educator.

00:00:30.848 --> 00:00:33.527
I make kinetic and robotic sculpture.

00:00:33.527 --> 00:00:36.136
And often these robotic devices respond

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:38.636
to some sort of natural input.

00:00:40.768 --> 00:00:43.189
Cloud Piano is a robotic installation

00:00:43.189 --> 00:00:46.018
that plays a piano based
on the movement and density

00:00:46.018 --> 00:00:48.629
of the clouds as they move across the sky.

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:50.319
As you can see, it's a device that mounts

00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:53.189
to the keyboard of a piano and

00:00:53.189 --> 00:00:54.599
there's another component, a camera,

00:00:54.599 --> 00:00:57.098
that's mounted outside the building here.

00:00:57.098 --> 00:00:59.978
And as clouds go past the camera,

00:00:59.978 --> 00:01:02.690
the video is sent in
real time to a computer,

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.317
that then scales the data.

00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:06.088
What I've done is taken the video feed

00:01:06.088 --> 00:01:08.648
from the camera and chopped it into

00:01:08.648 --> 00:01:10.497
88 individual pieces,

00:01:10.497 --> 00:01:13.418
as if they are the 88
keys on the keyboard.

00:01:13.418 --> 00:01:16.077
So as the clouds go past a particular key,

00:01:16.077 --> 00:01:17.698
that key is pressed.

00:01:17.698 --> 00:01:19.657
And so it's a simple algorithm, I suppose,

00:01:19.657 --> 00:01:22.968
that says if you see more white than blue

00:01:22.968 --> 00:01:25.458
in this little section of the video,

00:01:25.458 --> 00:01:27.125
then press that key.

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:30.538
I guess you could think
of it then as maybe

00:01:30.538 --> 00:01:32.789
a collaboration where I set up this

00:01:32.789 --> 00:01:35.298
certain situation and
I'm making these choices,

00:01:35.298 --> 00:01:38.798
but after that it's kinda up to the other.

00:01:41.387 --> 00:01:45.149
These odd sounds that
that the piano is making

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:49.316
continue to build or dissipate
or move and change shape.

00:01:50.716 --> 00:01:53.437
Machines, or computers, are
thought to be very orderly

00:01:53.437 --> 00:01:56.248
and very precise and
likewise nature is thought

00:01:56.248 --> 00:01:57.996
to be very chaotic.

00:01:57.996 --> 00:01:59.546
What keeps me interested is I think

00:01:59.546 --> 00:02:02.245
there's a lot of systematic ways to

00:02:02.245 --> 00:02:06.208
look at these things that we
associate with chaos.

00:02:06.208 --> 00:02:08.791
(piano noises)

